  "answer": "The practical approach is to clarify the goods or services and their classification, rather than force them into an inaccurate standard term. The relevant requirements differ between preparing an international application and responding to examination of an international registration designating Australia (IRDA).\n\n**Preparing the wording**\n• **Regulations:** Regulation 4.4(6)–(7), concerning applications for registration, requires published listing terms to be used ‘as far as practicable’. Where those terms cannot describe the goods or services, provide sufficient information for the Registrar to decide their classification. This provision is not itself the IRDA-specific rule. [TMR1995/r4.4]\n• **Manual practice for international applications:** Group the goods and services into Nice classes, put the class number before each group, and list the classes numerically. You need not copy the basic trade mark’s exact wording, but the international claim must not be broader than the basic trade mark’s claim. [TMM/Part60/2/2/2/2/4~4]\n• **Manual account of the international process:** The International Bureau (IB) controls classification; the Manual states that its opinion prevails in a classification dispute with the Office of origin. [TMM/Part60/4/6/4/6/2]\n\n**If the issue arises in an IRDA**\n• **Manual practice:** A provisional refusal may ask the holder to explain or limit vague, incomprehensible or linguistically incorrect wording. Respond by explaining or limiting the claim as requested; the Manual states that the terms will be refused if the holder does not respond. [TMM/Part60/4/6/4/6/2]\n• **Regulations:** Under regulation 17A.14(2), wording identified by the IB as defective cannot be used unless the Registrar is satisfied it is sufficiently clear in the circumstances. Regulation 17A.14(3) separately prohibits wording where the Registrar is reasonably satisfied it is too vague to determine protection’s scope, incomprehensible or linguistically incorrect. [TMR1995/r17A.14]\n\nThe supplied passages do not set out the detailed procedure or deadlines for correcting an IB classification irregularity. They therefore support the approach above, but not complete procedural instructions for that stage."
